---
title: Christmas Cake
source: https://www.recipetineats.com/christmas-cake/
serves: 20
prep time: 30 minutes
cook time: 3 hours 15 minutes
total time: 4 hours 45 minutes
tags:
  - dessert
  - cake
  - fruitcake
---

Combine the @raisins{300%g}, @dried apricots{150%g} (chopped into 1/3-inch pieces), @mixed peel{75%g} (diced into 1/5-inch pieces), @glacé cherries{150%g} (chopped into 1/3-inch pieces), @dates{180%g} (diced into 1/5-inch pieces), and @apple juice{1 1/8%cups} (or 1/3 cup brandy plus 2/3 cup plus 2 Tbsp apple juice) in a #large bowl{} that is microwave-safe.

Cover and #microwave{} on high for ~{1.5%minutes} until hot, then stir to coat all the fruit in liquid. Cover again and set aside for ~{1%hour} to plump up and cool.

Preheat the #oven{} to 320°F (160°C, or 140°C fan). Grease and line an 8- or 9-inch (21–22 cm) round #cake pan{} (at least 2.75 inches / 7 cm tall) with parchment paper.

Using a #hand mixer{}, beat the softened @unsalted butter{120%g} and packed @dark brown sugar{1.5%cups} until smooth and creamy, about ~{1%minute} on medium speed.

Add the @vegetable oil{1/2%cup} and @molasses{3%Tbsp} (or golden syrup) and beat until combined. Add the @salt{1/2%tsp}, @ground allspice{1/2%tsp}, @cinnamon{1/2%tsp}, @ground nutmeg{1/2%tsp}, and @baking powder{1/2%tsp}, beating until incorporated.

Add the @eggs{3%large}, one at a time, beating until just incorporated after each addition.

Stir in the @all-purpose flour{1 2/3%cups}. When mostly incorporated, fold in the soaked fruit mixture (including all remaining liquid in the bowl) and chopped @walnuts{3/4%cup} (optional).

Pour the batter into the prepared cake pan, cover tightly with #aluminum foil{}, and bake for ~{2.5%hours}. Remove the foil and bake for another 30 to ~{45%minutes}, until a skewer inserted into the center comes out clean with no wet batter attached (check at 30 minutes).

Remove from the oven and cool in the pan for ~{20%minutes} before transferring to a #wire rack{} to cool completely.

Serve plain, dusted with powdered sugar and topped with fresh cherries, or glazed with a plain sweet white glaze (or finished with marzipan and fondant). Pairs wonderfully with homemade pouring custard. Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 2–3 months, or freeze for up to 1 year.
